WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 194: Bill Clinton: The Boy from Hope, Oxford, and the Comeback Kid (1946–1993)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.640
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.640
[Rebecca] Today we step into the dynamic, charismatic world of Bill Clinton.

4
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:22.000
[Rebecca] With Clinton, a youthful new generation entered the White House, combining deep policy mastery with a down home southern warmth, saxophone solos, and an unquenchable political resilience.

5
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:30.960
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the boy from Hope, the JFK handshake, and the miracle of the comeback kid.

6
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:38.880
[George] William Jefferson Blythe III was born in August 1946 in the sleepy railroad town of Hope, Arkansas.

7
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:50.880
[George] Three months before his birth, his biological father, Bill Blythe, a handsome traveling equipment salesman, was killed when his car blew a tire and overturned into a waterfilled ditch.

8
00:00:50.880 --> 00:01:06.960
[George] Raised in his early years by his beloved grandmother Edith and grandfather Eldridge Cassidy, who ran a small grocery store where he treated African American customers with equal dignity despite Jim Crow customs, young Billy learned the virtues of racial tolerance.

9
00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:22.880
[George] His mother Virginia, an exuberant, fun-loving nurse anesthetist who loved horse racing, later married Roger Clinton, a local car dealership manager whose violent alcoholism cast a dark, terrifying shadow over their home in Hot Springs, Arkansas.

10
00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:33.760
[George] At age 14, standing over six feet tall, young Bill stepped between his stepfather and his mother, warning him firmly, You will never hit my mother again.

11
00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:45.360
[George] Bill excelled in school through ferocious intellect, becoming an all-state tenor saxophone player who practiced for hours every day, harboring a burning ambition to make his mark upon the world.

12
00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:55.760
[George] Growing up in Hope and Hot Springs, young Bill developed a ferocious resilience and deep empathy for the vulnerable, vowing to lift others from hardship through public service.

13
00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:04.680
[George] Growing up amid family hardship forged in Clinton a profound capacity to understand and share the feelings of everyday citizens from every walk of life.

14
00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:16.600
[George] In July 1963, 16-year-old Bill Clinton traveled to Washington, D.C., as an Arkansas delegate to American Legion Boys' Nation, a civic youth program.

15
00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:24.920
[George] On a blazing summer morning, July 24, the delegates were invited to the White House Rose Garden for a meeting with President John F.

16
00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:25.800
[George] Kennedy.

17
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:34.920
[George] As Kennedy emerged into the sunlight to deliver remarks, young Clinton maneuvered his way with relentless determination to the very front row of the rope line.

18
00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:40.440
[George] As Kennedy walked along the perimeter shaking hands, Clinton thrust his right hand forward.

19
00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:46.200
[George] Kennedy grasped it, looked directly into the Arkansas teenager's eyes, and smiled.

20
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:57.560
[George] A White House photographer snapped the black and white photograph, a radiant young JFK clasping hands with the tall, dark-haired teenager who would occupy the same office thirty years later.

21
00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:09.720
[George] Returning home to Hot Springs, Clinton showed the photograph to his mother, announcing with quiet, unshakable conviction that he was going to enter public life and become President of the United States.

22
00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:18.920
[George] Four months later, when Kennedy was assassinated in Dallas, Clinton wept in his bedroom, resolved to carry forward the torch of the new frontier.

23
00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:30.200
[George] That electric handshake in the Rose Garden with President Kennedy ignited a lifelong political flame, inspiring a gifted teenager to dedicate his life to the service of his country.

24
00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:39.480
[George] That iconic moment in the Rose Garden showed how a single brief encounter with a president can inspire a young person to pursue a life of public service.

25
00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:49.040
[George] Enrolling at Georgetown University in 1964, Clinton majored in international affairs, working part-time as a clerk for Arkansas Senator J.

26
00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:59.120
[George] William Fulbright, chairman of the Foreign Relations Committee, who mentored Clinton in the complexities of foreign policy and the tragedy of the Vietnam War.

27
00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:15.920
[George] Winning a coveted Rhodes scholarship to University College, Oxford, in 1968, Clinton spent two years traveling across Europe, devouring political philosophy, literature and history, while agonizing over his draft status during the Vietnam War.

28
00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:22.640
[George] In 1970, Clinton entered Yale Law School, where his life changed forever in the law library.

29
00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:31.520
[George] Noticing a brilliant, confident young woman with thick glasses and no makeup staring back at him across the room, Clinton watched her for several days.

30
00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:42.080
[George] Finally, the young woman closed her book, walked straight up to him, and said, If you're going to keep looking at me, and I'm going to keep looking back, we at least ought to know each other.

31
00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:43.840
[George] I'm Hilary Rodham.

32
00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:45.280
[George] What's your name?

33
00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:52.960
[George] It was the beginning of an intense, lifelong intellectual and political partnership that would reshape modern American politics.

34
00:04:52.960 --> 00:05:03.440
[George] His meeting with Hilary Rodham in the Yale Law Library marked the beginning of an extraordinary partnership of two brilliant legal minds who would reshape modern American politics.

35
00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:12.640
[George] His intellectual journey through Georgetown, Oxford, and Yale law prepared him with an encyclopedic grasp of domestic and international policy.

36
00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:19.200
[George] Returning home to Arkansas after law school, Clinton launched a meteoric ascent in state politics.

37
00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:27.440
[George] After narrowly losing an uphill congressional race in 1974, he was elected state attorney general in 1976.

38
00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:37.880
[George] Two years later, in 1978, at the age of 32, Clinton was elected governor of Arkansas, the youngest governor in the United States in forty years.

39
00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:40.600
[George] The national press hailed him as a wunderkind.

40
00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:49.720
[George] But Clinton's youthful arrogance and rapid push for educational reform and higher license plate fees provoked a fierce backlash from Arkansas voters.

41
00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:59.640
[George] In 1980, swept out by the Reagan landslide, Clinton was defeated after a single term, becoming the youngest former governor in American history.

42
00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:11.160
[George] Devastated, Clinton spent two years traveling to every general store, diner, and county fair in Arkansas, humbly apologizing to voters for moving too fast and not listening.

43
00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:17.400
[George] In 1982, the people of Arkansas forgave him, re electing Clinton to the governor's mansion.

44
00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:33.400
[George] Over the next decade, Clinton served four consecutive terms, radically improving Arkansas schools, attracting new businesses, and becoming the national leader of the New Democrats, advocating fiscal responsibility, welfare reform, and economic growth.

45
00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:44.600
[George] The painful loss and subsequent resurrection in Arkansas politics taught Clinton the vital lesson of political humility that leadership requires listening to the people as much as leading them.

46
00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:53.880
[George] The resurrection in Arkansas taught Clinton how to connect with voters on a deeply personal level, turning political setbacks into opportunities for renewal.

47
00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:59.080
[George] In October 1991, Clinton announced his candidacy for the presidency.

48
00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:12.280
[George] But in the snowy primary of New Hampshire in early 1992, his campaign was rocked by explosive tabloid allegations of an extramarital affair with Jennifer Flowers and scrutiny over his college draft status.

49
00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:15.920
[George] Lesser politicians would have collapsed under the media onslaught.

50
00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:18.800
[George] Clinton fought back with ferocious stamina.

51
00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:27.280
[George] Appearing with Hillary on 60 Minutes After the Super Bowl, Clinton confronted the rumors with candor, admitting he had caused pain in his marriage.

52
00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:39.120
[George] He spent 16 hour days shaking hands in New Hampshire bowling alleys and factory gates, climbing back from the dead to finish a strong second place, proudly declaring himself the comeback kid.

53
00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:43.760
[George] Then, in June 1992, trailing behind George H.W.

54
00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:51.600
[George] Bush and Ross Perrault, Clinton staged a cultural revolution, appearing on the late night Arsenio Hall show.

55
00:07:51.600 --> 00:08:01.360
[George] Forty-five-year-old Clinton donned dark rayband sunglasses and blew his tenor saxophone on a spirited rendition of Elvis Presley's Heartbreak Hotel.

56
00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:06.640
[George] The television moment shattered the stiff, traditional mold of presidential politics.

57
00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:12.240
[George] Connecting Clinton with young voters and minority audiences as a cool, accessible leader.

58
00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:22.240
[George] That spirited saxophone performance on late-night television signaled a cultural revolution in politics, connecting a new generation to the highest office in the land.

59
00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:31.600
[George] The saxophone appearance on late night television redefined political communication, bringing a fresh, relaxed energy to presidential campaigning.

60
00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:44.960
[George] Operating from a frantic, cinder block campaign headquarters in Little Rock known as the War Room, campaign strategists James Carville and George Stefanopoulos pioneered modern, rapid response political warfare.

61
00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:57.760
[George] On the wall, Carville scrawled three simple, disciplined rules: change versus more of the same, don't forget health care, and the immortal phrase that defined the election, the economy stupid.

62
00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:01.320
[George] Clinton picked Tennessee Senator Al Gore as his running mate.

63
00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:14.440
[George] Creating an energetic, youthful All-Southern ticket that barnstormed across America in a pair of shiny campaign buses, stopping spontaneously in small towns to eat cheeseburgers and talk with ordinary citizens.

64
00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:24.840
[George] During the three-way debates against President Bush and independent billionaire Ross Perrault, Clinton's masterly command of policy detail and natural empathy shone brightly.

65
00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:28.120
[George] In a town hall debate in Richmond, Virginia.

66
00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:42.440
[George] When a woman asked how the national debt affected the candidates personally, Clinton walked right up to the edge of the stage, looked into her eyes, asked about her family, and spoke with profound empathy about lost jobs and mortgage struggles.

67
00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:49.400
[George] On November 3, 1992, Clinton won 43 million votes, defeating George H.

68
00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:49.720
[George] W.

69
00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:52.920
[George] Bush and ending 12 years of Republican rule.

70
00:09:52.920 --> 00:10:04.360
[George] The 1992 campaign proved that laser-focused economic messaging paired with deep personal empathy could overcome any political deficit and unite a broad national coalition.

71
00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:13.560
[George] The 1992 campaign proved that a disciplined focus on the economic well-being of everyday families is the ultimate key to democratic success.

72
00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:27.720
[George] On January 20, 1993, 46-year-old Bill Clinton took the oath of office as the 42nd President of the United States, the first member of the baby boomer generation to reach the nation's highest office.

73
00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:38.680
[George] To the joyous, rhythmic beat of Fleetwood Mac's classic anthem, Don't Stop Thinking About Tomorrow, the crowds in Washington celebrated the dawn of a vibrant, youthful era.

74
00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:45.520
[George] In his inaugural address, Clinton summoned the nation to renewal with words that reflected his boundless optimism.

75
00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:50.240
[George] There is nothing wrong with America that cannot be cured by what is right with America.

76
00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:51.920
[George] We have forced the spring.

77
00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:58.080
[George] Today, we do not merely celebrate the imitation of the past, but the invention of the future.

78
00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:05.360
[George] The small town boy from Hope, Arkansas, who had stood in the Rose Garden 30 years earlier to shake the hand of John F.

79
00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:18.080
[George] Kennedy now sat behind the resolute desk in the Oval Office, prepared to lead America into an era of unprecedented technological expansion, economic prosperity, and generational change.

80
00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:28.560
[George] As Fleetwood Mack played into the Washington night, Bill Clinton brought the energy and idealism of the baby boomer generation into the presidency, promising a new beginning.

81
00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:38.960
[George] Bill Clinton's presidency began with a joyful summons to national renewal, ushering in an era of technological innovation, peace, and shared prosperity.

82
00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:48.400
[Rebecca] Bill Clinton captured the presidency through raw political resilience, personal charisma, and an instinctive connection to ordinary Americans.

83
00:11:48.400 --> 00:12:00.480
[Rebecca] But the White House brought fierce political battles with Newt Gingrich's Republican Revolution, government shutdowns, and a sensational personal scandal that culminated in presidential impeachment.

84
00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:02.320
[Rebecca] Next week, we conclude the U.S.

85
00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:06.240
[Rebecca] president series with Bill Clinton's second term, George W.

86
00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:14.720
[Rebecca] Bush, Barack Obama, Donald Trump, and Joe Biden, completing our epic 90 episode Chronicle of the American presidency.

87
00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:19.200
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

